{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for the ordinary evidence-only DESIGN review of revision 21 at exact b68ce5e3c2c98e6064fbb8af8859bf4020fcc42c, tree df1bcef4068ec5780e5c7188f4a3bcfcec2d9ef2. Primary ef1fbf05 was examined first, with all four canonical artifacts, under invitation f198dfb8 and checker promise 376fdb6d. This is evidence for request 22fc1a93 / builder promise cdc93e34; it confers no adoption, source approval, merge authority or implementation closure.\n\nBoth reservation findings from revision 20 now have explicit repairs. Two new design conflicts need correction before this contract is accepted: the binding form cannot express the adopted withdrawal selector, and independent settlement marks have conflicting reservation grouping rules. The second is a specification conflict, not a proved unconditional one-entry admission or runtime overflow.\n\nCoverage: all four current bodies were freshly read collectively through EOF, including the unchanged inventory and lane companion: 1,958,195 bytes / 22,022 LF. Both complete changed native U3 deltas total 170,521 bytes / 2,002 LF; the other two deltas are exactly empty. Five disjoint primary ranges cover 0..1447902, and three corrections ranges cover 0..301884. Eighteen assigned units have 248 actual, gap-free displays, each at most 9000 characters and at most two per call. All eight whole-body/delta unions and four native blob/body/delta checks pass. Root personally read all five complete reports/ledgers, the four finding/qualification supplements and their ledgers, the final mark-closure countercheck, the current 17.2a and 6.4/AlsoRule context, and fresh adopted R26 withdrawal/reservation context. No root personal FULL claim is made for the entire primary, corrections or R26. Thirty-two supplementary source units and fifteen root source intervals were independently verified, with no identity/gap failures. Final seal: /tmp/artroom-checker-r1-r21-b68c-ordinary-root-binding-v2.json, 82658 bytes, SHA256 f42caeff6951b851d28ee310f5a7e467354277bf089dba0c066c9fac8d862a25. One initial root display of 10103 characters exceeded the slice convention; it was reread as compliant 9000+1103 slices and the deviation is retained in the seal. No truncation or earlier-head coverage credit is claimed.\n\nP2 1 — Make the binding form express the adopted rightful withdrawal.\nCurrent section 17.2a promises a member's own pending withdrawal can be decided with no free room (15569–15575), but its validator requires bound.of to be an also name selected by a WRITTEN rule, with no mark (15599–15608). Adopted R26 has one operation field in withdraw, a foreign change-lane merge fact. Its destination has many publication items, and P15's publication-of selector at place 2 finds the local publication whose operation equals that field (R26 9793–9808 and 11772).\n\nThe written AlsoRule grammar, R21 5203–5207 / 5297–5331, has no equivalent. by names a local item or the local item opened by a local fact; a foreign merge fact is neither. via follows a slot from an already resolved local subject. branch.slot and branch.judging cannot identify an arbitrary queued target: with two queued publications, the withdrawal may be for the other one. one applies only to a non-many type; making publications a singleton would remove required concurrency.\n\nThe required publication-of mark selects the right item, but the new bound validator rejects it. Without bound, the request remains new work and can wait at capacity. Listing R3's decisions/bound rows as owed does not supply the missing selector; those rows cannot express this jointly required request with the stated grammar.\n\nSupply a bounded, deterministic local binding selector, or permit the existing pure selector mark under explicit input/type/work restrictions. A jointly reviewed message/form change is another route only if it preserves arbitrary queued withdrawals, sender ownership, unknown-before-reserve behavior, repeat handling and concurrent publications. Keep binding independent of external observations and keep every typed-field, own-lane, nonfinal and positive-count condition. Add a compact two-queued-publications witness at a full destination, with the target outside branch.slot/judging and wrong-owner/repeat controls. This is a design mappability finding, not a currently deployed destination defect.\n\nP2 2 — Define reservation identity and finite closure for independent settlement marks.\nThe new 6.4 rule permits distinct required/default-false/nonfixed bool marks on one item and one settles declaration per form. It then says several forms settling one ITEM use the largest reservation (5684–5686). DI18 counts the states an effect list can set, and again maximizes alternatives for one state (15128–15139). The new reconciliation row 5718 also says to exclude the completed own mark while counting the subject's remaining state settlements. These rules do not define how distinct, independently consumable marker duties are grouped or how completed marks propagate through the closure.\n\nUse the minimal shape of a live open item with marks a and b, and two ordinary acts: one settles a and writes only a=true; the other settles b and writes only b=true. Neither changes state, sets a deadline, sends, opens a duty or uses a platform code mark. The shape meets the stated mark restrictions, and setting a leaves b pending.\n\nUnder the literal item-wide maximum with ordinary value effects starting no new state/deadline duty, the two one-entry settlements can be merged into one reservation; writing the first while retaining the second would increase Used+Reserved by one. A stronger reading of row 5718 can count the remaining mark in each closure and reserve two instead. That is real counterevidence: the review has not proved every complete validator admits the shape with one reserved entry, nor an unconditional overflow. A naive recursion that assumes every other mark false at each call can also invent an A→B→A cycle, though only-true marks cannot actually recreate completed duties. No evaluator or history was executed.\n\nThe contract needs one determinate finite rule, consistent with both the admission formula and the claimed settling guarantee. Define a marker duty by (item, slot); maximize only alternative forms for that same duty, sum distinct marker duties and the separate state duties, and state how completed/already-reserved markers affect DI18 and cycle validation. Preserve multiple-marker functionality. Add the two-marker case in both orders, one mark already true, two alternatives for the SAME mark, and a state settlement beside the marks. Witness 18.51's single mark does not distinguish these cases.\n\nPrior repairs: held one(k) now includes the finite unheld C(j) for each possible outcome, in all dimensions, and transfers it from holder to operation to child duty. The 14-entry K/U example properly includes both possible outcome entries; the former 2-entry reserve fails its control. Result-clause observation-named values now have explicit domain/max declarations, count for every applicable form, and are reserved at request admission using the largest complete terminal-clause amount. A diagnosis retains the later result reservation; actual domain/digest dedup releases only unused room. These are sound explicit repairs of the two prior omissions, without accepting their implementation or numerical budgets.\n\nArchitecture and breadth: declaration-driven retained-value accounting and one/req/itm/dec transfers are useful general forms. The five-dimension and observes-place sweeps expose more interfaces, but a sweep table is not a proof that every jointly required selector or duty identity is expressible. Keep the owner rows, late-answer obligations, full application scope and the source work distinct from this design verdict. Estimates remove no functionality.\n\nSecurity: counts grant no permission. Bound refusals may consume a folded decision count without adding a handler effect, while guards still decide the request. Missing observation/value bytes remain absent, not positive authority. Source scope/incarnation/head, row status, windows/reuse/prior/run and signer/controller restrictions remain required. Unknown attempts keep late-answer closures and block reset; a read or final holder does not infer an own answer. Fault-status reads, operator recovery and member display are still owned by the authority note and lane forms.\n\nNonblocking clarification: check 5 says an unheld outcome mark lists no held kind and also says no held kind is reached from an unheld kind. Make the latter explicitly cover request/result-clause and every other closure edge if that is the intended validation condition. An unheld U has no for; its request q inherits no holder account; release can otherwise free H's V count before q's clause draws it. The broad transitive no-reach rule may already prohibit that path, so no unconditional third P2 is filed. Keep the new graph restriction visible and map every required owner/application flow; a restriction alone is not proof of complete functionality. Prefer explicit transferable claims if a required flow needs the edge.\n\nOther clarifications: mirror state-change closure amounts in the Items/Records/Pending requests cells of one(k), which the fuller prose already counts. Keep historical smaller formulas visibly replaced. The revised Report.name and overlapping whole/absent row rules answer the earlier wording concerns. First-outcome-only child opening remains a declared/validated future property, never inferred.\n\nPlanner assertion 39749e26 was read in full. It accepts the three R26 section 6.5 changes: typed reserve-message facts, counting at reserve delivery, and fetching/retaining even nonrequired job entries. Worst-case state counting is accepted; a more selective declaration is future optimization. Final-publication withdraw is new work; the own pending request is the reserved case. Authority owns fault read/record/report; lanes own member display. These are explicit owned decisions, not an already updated adopted R26 or source. They do not resolve the two new conflicts above.\n\nSimplification and scalability: give each reservation the same identity as the independently completed duty, and reuse one constrained local selection rule for binding. Avoid runtime flags, fabricated input evidence or a narrower form merely to make conservation easy. Conservative maxima and bounded two-step observation discovery remain proposals; charged work, all five capacity dimensions, platform maxima and actual worst-case records/bytes remain owed.\n\nNative changes are only the primary and corrections docs. Packages tree 37db82f91aa0ab8ba0ce7d8457918cffc07640ce is identical at b68c and parent 9dbd. No project gate or execution is claimed. R1r19/R26/R2r15 remain standing; R20 and R21 are not adopted by this review. The separately accepted M3 source milestone does not implement these forms. All remaining I3/IA/I4/I5/E1, browser/CLI/device/agent, workspace/save/publication, host/runner/storage/Worker/clock/deployment/live witnesses, retained custody and ordinary independent review duties remain in complete scope.\n\nValidation was immutable native Git, builtin static text/hash/count, /tmp evidence and normal gitseq only. No project imports/execution/tests/gate/build/install/typecheck/evaluator/simulation/generated histories/private diagnostics/provider/contact/process/source edits/merge/push; forbidden cloud-review/static-ledger bodies were never opened. Final prepare confirms evidence-only for 22fc1a93, primary first and all four artifacts, with no implementations supplied.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ef1fbf054f5027c11597950fddd7bb46fccdf1fa","binding":"evidence-only","head":"b68ce5e3c2c98e6064fbb8af8859bf4020fcc42c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:376fdb6d399fa8223742e77af396d2885c754e0e\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c66885ac9219b2b1d66e945e9345f2cdd709fc28","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}